WEBVTT

n01
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:06.650
This is for when you’re lying in bed and you just can’t fall asleep.

n02
00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:13.170
Or you wake up in the middle of the night and can’t get back to sleep.

n03
00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.190
It’s called the half-hour rule,

n04
00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:19.390
and it’s the single most useful thing I’ve gotten from CBTi:

n05
00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:21.910
cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia.

n06
00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:23.560
Here’s what you do.

n07
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.120
After lying awake for 20 to 30 minutes, get out of bed.

n08
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.780
Don’t watch a clock.

n09
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.840
Get up when you have a rough sense it’s been that long.

n10
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.310
Go into the next room and do something relaxing.

n11
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.790
Maybe read a book, or listen to a podcast.

n12
00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:45.400
It’s best not to look at your phone.

n13
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.320
Then, after 20 to 30 minutes, or as soon as you get drowsy,

n14
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.430
go back to bed and see what happens.

n15
00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.450
Still awake? Get up again.

n16
00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:59.060
Repeat as many times as you need to.

n17
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.620
When we lie in bed trying to will ourselves to become sleepy,

n18
00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.010
all that does is create anxiety.

n19
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:10.340
The more we resist sleeplessness, the more we entrench it.

n20
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.450
So the best thing is to stop resisting.

n21
00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:17.650
Just accept that you’re not sleepy right now, for whatever reason.

n22
00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.440
No big deal.

n23
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.820
It may seem like a paradox,

n24
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.660
but by giving up on falling asleep right this second,

n25
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.000
you give yourself more of a chance to fall asleep later.

